Infants develop self-recognition (mirror test) at 18 months — well before they can articulate a Cogito — suggesting self-awareness is a deep biological feature, not a philosophical construct.
Buddhist philosophy independently arrived at the question of whether the 'self' that thinks can doubt itself — different tradition, same puzzle, suggesting the Cogito addresses a universal feature of consciousness.
Artificial intelligence systems that lack genuine self-awareness cannot apply the Cogito to themselves — they can output the words but the self-certifying certainty is unavailable, illustrating why the argument depends on genuine first-person experience.
Descartes' insight anticipated modern philosophy of mind debates about qualia and subjective experience — his argument is the ancestor of Nagel's 'what is it like to be a bat?' and Chalmers' 'hard problem of consciousness.'
The certainty Descartes sought — a foundation immune to sceptical attack — is available only for first-person mental states; even the best scientific knowledge is revisable, but the existence of the reviser is not.
